            <ArticleTitle>Pain among older adults from a gender perspective: findings from the Swedish National Study on Aging and Care (SNAC-Blekinge).</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>258-63</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1177/1403494815618842</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND AND AIMS" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">Pain is common in the elderly population and its prevalence varies according to the studied disease, clinical setting, sex and age. This study examines pain in an aging population from a gender perspective.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">The Swedish National study on Aging and Care (SNAC) is conducted at four research centres. Participants were recruited from the baseline sample (n=1402) at one of the research centres, SNAC-Blekinge. Individuals aged 60 years and older were included and non-participation was documented. Research personnel conducted the medical examination on two occasions.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The prevalence of pain was 769/1402 (54.8%), distributed as 496/817 (64.5%) women and 273/585 (35.5%) men,p&lt;0.01. Women reported more pain located in the vertebral column,p&lt;0.01. The most common pain location was the legs and feet. About 84% reported pain intensity as 4 or higher on the visual analogue scale (VAS). Pain intensity declines with age among men,p&lt;0.01. The most frequent treatment was painkillers. A total of 128/263 (48.7%) of the men received no pain treatment compared with 177/478 (37.0%) of the women,p&lt;0.01. In a multivariate logistic regression model, women yielded the highest OR [OR 1.94 (C.I. 1.51-2.49)] for pain.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Pain is common among older adults and there are significant differences between the sexes. Almost 55% of participants reported pain, predominantly women. In the majority of cases the intensity was rated as moderate or severe (VAS &gt;4) and women rated higher than men p&lt;002. Almost half of the men (48.7%) did not receive any treatment compared to 37% of the women, p&lt;001.</AbstractText>
